Shanghai Guanlong Valve Machinery Co., Ltd. v. Tang Maolin — dispute over labor contract

【Ruling Summary】

Where an employer has a clear requirement for the academic qualifications of applicants during recruitment, and an applicant provides a false academic certificate and signs a labor contract with the employer, this constitutes a circumstance under Article 26 of the *Labor Contract Law* where a labor contract entered into by means of fraud shall be invalid, and the employer may rescind such labor contract in accordance with Article 39 of the *Labor Contract Law*.

Plaintiff: Shanghai Guanlong Valve Machinery Co., Ltd., domiciled at Deyuan Road, Nanxiang Town, Jiading District, Shanghai.

Legal Representative: Li Zhenghong, Chairman of the Company.

Defendant: Tang Maolin, male, 31 years old, domiciled at Gaoling Township, Susong County, Anqing City, Anhui Province.

The plaintiff, Shanghai Guanlong Valve Machinery Co., Ltd. (hereinafter referred to as Guanlong Company), filed a lawsuit with the People's Court of Jiading District, Shanghai, against the defendant, Tang Maolin, regarding a dispute over a labor contract.

Plaintiff Guanlong Company alleged: The defendant, Tang Maolin, by providing a false academic certificate and using fraudulent misrepresentations in March 2002, induced the plaintiff company to sign a labor contract with him against its true intention. Subsequently, each time the plaintiff required employees to update their personnel records, the defendant filled in false information by deception. After receiving relevant reports, the company verified the above facts and also discovered that the defendant had engaged in misconduct during his work by falsely reporting contract prices to pocket the差价, causing economic losses to the company and damaging its reputation. In light of this, the plaintiff terminated the labor contract with the defendant. In July and August 2010, the defendant and the plaintiff respectively filed for arbitration with the Shanghai Jiading District Labor Dispute Arbitration Commission (hereinafter referred to as the Jiading District Labor Arbitration Commission). On September 17, 2010, the Jiading District Labor Arbitration Commission issued the award Jiayang Zhong (2010) Ban Zi Nos. 1860 and 2188, some contents of which did not conform to the law and facts. The defendant fraudulently induced the plaintiff to sign the labor contract, rendering the contract void, and he also seriously violated the company's rules and regulations. Therefore, the plaintiff's act constituted a lawful rescission, and the plaintiff should not pay the defendant compensation for unlawful termination of the labor contract. Furthermore, as the defendant did not complete the second quarter of 2010, the plaintiff should not pay him the bonus for that quarter. Accordingly, the plaintiff requested the court to: 1. Not order the plaintiff to pay the defendant compensation for unlawful termination of the labor contract in the amount of RMB 181,866; 2. Not order the plaintiff to pay the defendant the second quarter bonus for 2010 in the amount of RMB 32,213; 3. Order the defendant to return to the plaintiff the temporary advances and loans totaling RMB 28,500 taken from the company between December 2009 and May 2010 (including RMB 10,000 temporarily advanced for business expenses, a loan of RMB 15,000, RMB 2,000 temporarily advanced for tea leaves, and RMB 1,500 temporarily advanced in the name of a workstation rent deposit); 4. Order the defendant to return to the plaintiff the rental payment of RMB 8,190; 5. Order the defendant to refund three months' car insurance premium of RMB 697.75. The plaintiff also expressed its acceptance of the other contents of the arbitration award.

Plaintiff Guanlong Company submitted the following evidence:

1. The arbitration award Jiayang Zhong (2010) Ban Zi Nos. 1860 and 2188, to establish that the case had undergone the mandatory arbitration procedure;
2. The labor contract and supplementary agreement, to establish the existence of a labor relationship between the parties;
3. Four temporary advance vouchers, to establish that the defendant Tang Maolin had taken four advances/loans from the plaintiff between December 2009 and May 2010, totaling RMB 28,500;
4. A payment voucher, to establish that the plaintiff Guanlong Company had paid the rent of RMB 81,900 for the period from May 22 to November 21, 2010, to the defendant Tang Maolin, but the defendant subsequently failed to provide a lease contract;
5. Two purchase and sale contracts, a payment certificate issued by Su Pump Industry Co., Ltd., a cash check stub, and a record of an interview between the plaintiff's lawyer and the buyer's manager, Tian Dong, each one copy, to establish that the defendant Tang Maolin had entered into "dual contracts" (yin-yang contracts) with clients and pocketed the difference;
6. A copy of the academic certificate submitted by the defendant Tang Maolin to the plaintiff Guanlong Company and a certificate from the Academic Affairs Office of Xi'an University of Technology, to establish that the defendant Tang Maolin had fabricated his academic qualifications;
7. A commitment letter upon taking office, to establish that the defendant Tang Maolin had promised that "if any personal materials provided to the company are found to be false, I am willing to be unconditionally terminated," and therefore the plaintiff Guanlong Company lawfully rescinded the labor contract;
8. The 2007 edition of the *Employee Handbook* and the *Acknowledgement Confirmation* signed by the defendant, to establish that the plaintiff Guanlong Company rescinded the labor contract with the defendant Tang Maolin in accordance with the company's rules and regulations;
9. A written statement by Ma Yuxin, former manager of the East China Business Department of the plaintiff Guanlong Company, and Ma Yuxin's job transfer documents, to establish that although Ma Yuxin had learned of the defendant Tang Maolin's false academic qualifications in August 2008, he did not report it to the company or take action due to his job transfer;
10. A car insurance payment voucher, to establish that the plaintiff Guanlong Company paid RMB 2,791 in car insurance premiums for the defendant Tang Maolin;
11. The *Recruitment and Reward/Punishment System* and the 2003 edition of the *Employee Handbook*, to establish that the plaintiff Guanlong Company required its business personnel to have at least a college degree.

Defendant Tang Maolin argued: Although the defendant provided a false academic certificate when joining the plaintiff Guanlong Company, the plaintiff company had already learned the truth in 2008 and had dealt with the defendant accordingly. Moreover, in December 2008, the plaintiff renewed the labor contract with the defendant, which was sufficient to prove that the plaintiff, considering the defendant's strong business capabilities, chose not to hold his false academic qualifications against him. Therefore, when the plaintiff rescinded the labor contract with the defendant in June 2010 on the grounds of false academic qualifications, it violated the law, and the plaintiff should pay the defendant compensation for unlawful termination of the labor contract. The plaintiff sent the defendant a lawyer's letter on June 30, 2010, notifying him of the rescission of the labor contract; thus, the labor relationship between the parties ended at the end of June 2010. The defendant had completed the second quarter, and the plaintiff should pay him the bonus for that quarter. Regarding the temporarily advanced funds and loan of RMB 28,500 mentioned by the plaintiff: RMB 6,922 of the RMB 10,000 business funds had already been spent, so the defendant agreed to return RMB 3,078; the RMB 15,000 was a loan the defendant intended to take from the plaintiff in his personal capacity, but because the leader did not sign and approve it, the defendant did not actually receive the loan and therefore disagreed with returning it; the RMB 2,000 for tea leaves was used by the defendant to give tea leaves to a client on behalf of his leader, Yang Chengxin. The leader asked the defendant to temporarily advance RMB 2,000 from the company and then settle it with invoices, but the defendant had not yet found invoices to do so; of the RMB 1,500 temporarily advanced in the name of workstation rent, RMB 1,300 was a rent deposit and RMB 182 was for electricity. As the plaintiff's termination of the labor relationship with the defendant led to an early termination of the lease for the workstation's office space, resulting in a breach of contract, the defendant should not have to return these two amounts; he now agreed to return the remaining RMB 18. The rent of RMB 8,190 for the period from May to November 2010 was paid after approval by the plaintiff's deputy general manager, so the defendant disagreed with returning it. The defendant agreed to return the three months' car insurance premium of RMB 697.75. Furthermore, as the plaintiff had paid urban social insurance for the defendant in Wuxi based on the minimum contribution base, which violated the law, the defendant requested that the plaintiff pay him the difference in urban social insurance contributions in Wuxi from July 2008 to July 2010 based on his actual income as the contribution base.

Defendant Tang Maolin submitted the following evidence:

1. Audio recordings of conversations between the defendant Tang Maolin and Ma Yuxin, and between Tang Maolin and Pan Rujuan, each one copy, to establish that the plaintiff Guanlong Company had already dealt with the matter of the defendant's false academic qualifications as early as 2008;
2. An electricity bill, to establish that the electricity fee payable for the defendant Tang Maolin's workstation in June 2010 was RMB 182, which was included in the RMB 1,500 temporarily advanced by the defendant from the plaintiff Guanlong Company on May 31, 2010;
3. Two payment vouchers, one travel expense report, and six invoices, to establish that after temporarily advancing RMB 10,000 in business funds from the plaintiff Guanlong Company in December 2009, the defendant Tang Maolin had spent a total of RMB 6,922 on entertainment, meals, travel, etc.

Facts Found by the People's Court of Jiading District, Shanghai, at First Instance:

The defendant, Tang Maolin, was a migrant worker in Shanghai. On March 1, 2002, Tang Maolin began working in sales for the plaintiff Guanlong Company. Upon joining, Tang Maolin submitted to Guanlong Company's human resources department a copy of an academic certificate purporting to show his graduation from the Department of Materials Engineering of Xi'an University of Technology in July 2000. The parties signed a labor contract for the period from March 1, 2002, to December 31, 2002, with a probationary period from March 1, 2002, to August 1, 2002. Thereafter, the parties renewed the labor contract annually for one-year terms. On December 25, 2007, Tang Maolin signed a *Commitment Letter upon Taking Office*, which stated: "As an employee of Shanghai Guanlong Valve Machinery Co., Ltd., I hereby make the following commitment: ... All personal materials I have previously provided to the company are true and valid. If any are false, I am willing to be unconditionally terminated..." On December 23, 2008, the parties signed a *Supplementary Agreement to the Labor Contract*, extending the original labor contract's validity period to December 31, 2011. Guanlong Company had offices in both Nanjing and Wuxi, and later established a workstation in Changzhou (under the jurisdiction of the Wuxi office), with Tang Maolin serving as the workstation head. The workstation had no other employees. Guanlong Company allowed Tang Maolin to lease office space in his own name for work purposes, with the rental costs borne by the company. On October 25, 2009, Guanlong Company paid Tang Maolin car insurance premiums of RMB 2,791. On December 25, 2009, Tang Maolin submitted a temporary advance voucher (signed by department head Yang Chengxin) and withdrew RMB 10,000 for business expenses. On January 7, 2010, Tang Maolin spent a total of RMB 6,922 on entertainment, meals, travel, etc. On February 11, 2010, Tang Maolin filled out a temporary advance voucher stating the reason as "loan" for an amount of RMB 15,000, but this voucher was not signed and approved by the supervising leader. On April 23, 2010, Guanlong Company paid Tang Maolin rent (including taxes) of RMB 8,190 for the period from May 22, 2010, to November 21, 2010. On May 31, 2010, Tang Maolin filled out two temporary advance vouchers, signed by department head Yang Chengxin, and withdrew RMB 1,500 for the workstation's rent deposit and RMB 2,000 for tea leaves from Guanlong Company. The voucher for the tea leaves bore the notation "find invoices to offset the account." Subsequently, Tang Maolin did not submit invoices for the tea leaves to Guanlong Company. On June 28, 2010, Guanlong Company issued a certificate of termination of employment to Tang Maolin, but Tang Maolin refused to accept it. On July 2, 2010, Tang Maolin received a lawyer's letter from Guanlong Company, which stated: "In view of the fact that the materials and statements you provided to Guanlong Company during your job application were false, and during your work period you failed to meet the business targets set by the company and did not comply with the company's work discipline and rules, Guanlong Company hereby dismisses you effective immediately, i.e., rescinds the labor contract with you." The letter was dated June 30, 2010. Guanlong Company did not pay Tang Maolin the remaining balance of the fourth quarter 2009 bonus (commission) of RMB 20,493.89, the first quarter 2010 bonus (commission) of RMB 1,198.40, or the second quarter 2010 bonus (commission) of RMB 32,213. Furthermore, in August 2008, Tang Maolin's immediate supervisor, Ma Yuxin (Manager of Guanlong Company's East China Business Department), learned of and confirmed the falsity of Tang Maolin's academic qualifications through a report from another person. After December 1, 2008, due to a job transfer, the region under Tang Maolin's responsibility was no longer under Ma Yuxin's management. During the labor arbitration stage, Guanlong Company stated that, in practice, when hiring employees for the offices, verification and guarantee were carried out by the office director, who then provided copies of employees' academic certificates to the company.

On November 1, 2010, the Academic Affairs Office of Xi'an University of Technology wrote on a copy of the defendant Tang Maolin's graduation certificate provided by the plaintiff Guanlong Company the certification note "This person is not found among the 2000 graduates" and affixed its seal. The Guanlong Company *Employee Handbook* contained the following provisions: "Newly recruited employees shall submit the originals of the following documents for verification by the human resources department at the time of reporting, and simultaneously submit one copy for filing: (1) ID card; (2) Academic certificate..." "If an employee falls under any of the following circumstances constituting a serious violation of company rules and regulations, the company will dismiss them without providing any economic compensation: ... Falsely reporting professional qualifications or other work experience by fraudulent means..." Tang Maolin had signed to acknowledge his awareness of the above contents. On July 19, 2010, and August 11, 2010, Tang Maolin and Guanlong Company respectively filed for arbitration with the Jiading District Labor Arbitration Commission regarding matters including compensation for unlawful termination of the labor contract and the return of temporarily advanced funds. On September 17, 2010, the Jiading District Labor Arbitration Commission issued the award Jiayang Zhong (2010) Ban Zi Nos. 1860 and 2188, with the following contents: 1. Guanlong Company shall pay the defendant, within one time, compensation for unlawful termination of the labor contract of RMB 181,866, the balance of the fourth quarter 2009 bonus (commission) of RMB 20,493.89, the first quarter 2010 bonus (commission) of RMB 1,198.40, and the second quarter 2010 bonus (commission) of RMB 32,213, totaling RMB 235,771.29; 2. Tang Maolin shall return to Guanlong Company, within one time, the temporary advance for business expenses dated December 25, 2009, of RMB 10,000, the temporary advance for purchasing tea leaves dated May 31, 2010, of RMB 2,000, the workstation rent deposit temporarily advanced on May 31, 2010, of RMB 1,500, the loan dated February 11, 2010, of RMB 15,000, and the car insurance premium of RMB 697.75, totaling RMB 29,197.75; 3. Other claims by Tang Maolin were not supported; 4. Other claims by Guanlong Company were not supported. Guanlong Company, dissatisfied with parts of the arbitration award, initiated this lawsuit.

The main issue in dispute at first instance was: Whether the defendant Tang Maolin's act of submitting a false academic certificate to the plaintiff Guanlong Company upon joining constituted one of the grounds for Guanlong Company to lawfully rescind the labor contract.

The People's Court of Jiading District, Shanghai, held at first instance:

One of the standards for determining fraud is whether the other party was aware of the true circumstances. Ma Yuxin of the plaintiff Guanlong Company was the manager of the business department that managed all offices in East China. His acts in recruiting and dismissing employees in the offices under his jurisdiction were official acts on behalf of the company. In December 2008, when Ma Yuxin was already aware that the defendant Tang Maolin had provided false academic qualifications, he nevertheless made the decision to renew the labor contract with him. This indicated that Guanlong Company, having already known about Tang Maolin's false academic qualifications, continued to employ him, i.e., it chose not to pursue the matter of Tang Maolin providing false academic qualifications. Furthermore, Guanlong Company's setting of academic qualification requirements for sales personnel was presumably to ensure their work capabilities. The fact that Tang Maolin had been continuously renewing his labor contract with Guanlong Company since joining in 2002 also indirectly confirmed that Guanlong Company recognized Tang Maolin's work capabilities. Therefore, Guanlong Company's claim that Tang Maolin had committed fraud could not be sustained, and Guanlong Company's rescission of the labor contract with him constituted an unlawful termination.

Accordingly, in accordance with Article 3, Paragraph 1 of Article 30, Article 47, and Article 87 of the *Labor Contract Law of the People's Republic of China*, Paragraph 1 of Article 64 of the *Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China*, and Article 2 of the *Supreme People's Court's Provisions on Evidence in Civil Proceedings*, the People's Court of Jiading District, Shanghai, on March 21, 2011, entered judgment as follows:

1. The plaintiff Guanlong Company shall, within ten days of this judgment taking effect, pay the defendant Tang Maolin compensation for unlawful termination of the labor contract of RMB 181,866, the balance of the fourth quarter 2009 bonus (commission) of RMB 20,493.89, the first quarter 2010 bonus (commission) of RMB 1,198.40, and the second quarter 2010 bonus (commission) of RMB 32,213. The total of the above four items is RMB 235,771.29;

2. The defendant Tang Maolin shall, within ten days of this judgment taking effect, return to the plaintiff Guanlong Company the temporary advance for business expenses dated December 25, 2009, of RMB 3,078, the workstation rent deposit temporarily advanced on May 31, 2010, of RMB 1,318, the workstation rental payment withdrawn on April 23, 2010, of RMB 6,370, and the car insurance premium of RMB 697.75. The total of the above four items is RMB 11,463.75;

3. The other claims of the plaintiff Guanlong Company are dismissed.

Guanlong Company, dissatisfied with the judgment at first instance, filed an appeal with the Shanghai No. 2 Intermediate People's Court. Guanlong Company appealed, arguing: 1. The termination of the labor contract by Guanlong Company with the appellee Tang Maolin was lawful and justified, and no compensation for illegal termination should be paid. First, Tang Maolin had engaged in fraudulent conduct by forging his educational qualifications; Tang Maolin used fraudulent means to induce Guanlong Company to enter into the labor contract. Under Guanlong Company's internal rules and the agreement between the parties, Guanlong Company's termination of the labor contract with him was lawful. When recruiting employees, Guanlong Company required applicants to submit original academic certificates. Tang Maolin, when applying in 2002, also submitted a forged academic certificate. Guanlong Company now only retains a copy of the academic certificate provided by Tang Maolin; the original was returned to Tang Maolin himself. Guanlong Company argued that even if it had failed to adequately verify the academic certificate when hiring Tang Maolin, this could not offset Tang Maolin's liability for forging his academic qualifications. Additionally, Manager Ma Yuxin of Guanlong Company was not the business manager for the entire East China region; he was only the business manager for the Suzhou area and lacked the authority to impose personnel sanctions. Even if Manager Ma Yuxin knew about Tang Maolin's forged academic certificate, this did not mean the company knew of the matter and had dealt with it. In fact, Ma Yuxin had also proven that he did not report the matter to the company. When Guanlong Company renewed the labor contract with Tang Maolin, Manager Ma Yuxin had already been transferred to another post, and Guanlong Company was unaware of Tang Maolin's forged academic qualifications at the time of renewal. Second, Tang Maolin had engaged in other acts of self-dealing during his employment. Tang Maolin had entered into a dual contract with Changzhou Subeng Pump Industry Co., Ltd.; the contract given to the client stipulated a price of RMB 2,115, while the contract submitted to the company was only RMB 1,407, and the actual cash received by Guanlong Company was only RMB 1,407. Changzhou Subeng Pump Industry Co., Ltd. had also issued a certificate to this effect. Such self-dealing by Tang Maolin seriously violated the company's internal rules. Guanlong Company's termination of the labor contract with Tang Maolin was in accordance with the law, and Guanlong Company was not required to pay Tang Maolin compensation for illegal termination of the labor contract. The court of first instance erred in fact-finding and incorrectly applied the law. Guanlong Company requested that the first instance judgment's first item regarding compensation for illegal termination of the labor contract be reversed, and that the court rule that Guanlong Company did not have to pay Tang Maolin compensation for illegal termination of the labor contract in the amount of RMB 181,866. 2. The court of first instance's finding regarding Tang Maolin's obligation to repay the company RMB 15,000 and RMB 10,000 under the advance payment slips did not match the actual facts. Guanlong Company argued that both sums should be fully repaid. Regarding the RMB 15,000 advance payment slip, Guanlong Company argued that Tang Maolin had left a signed loan slip with Guanlong Company, indicating that Tang Maolin had actually received the aforementioned sum. The court of first instance lacked a basis for finding that Tang Maolin had not actually received this sum. Regarding the RMB 10,000 advance payment slip, Guanlong Company's rules required employees to submit business expense reimbursements to their supervisors for review within one month after the expense was incurred. Tang Maolin only claimed this business expense reimbursement during the arbitration proceedings, which was contrary to the rules. Thus, the full amount should be repaid. Guanlong Company requested that the first instance judgment's second item be reversed and that Tang Maolin be ordered to fully repay Guanlong Company the advance payment of RMB 25,000.

During the second instance, the appellant Guanlong Company submitted two sets of new evidence:

1. An autobiography written by the appellee Tang Maolin when he joined the company in 2002, in which he stated that he graduated from the Department of Materials Engineering of Xi'an Institute of Technology, intended to prove that Tang Maolin made false statements regarding his educational background at the time of joining the company;

2. Two personnel information cards of the appellant Guanlong Company, filled out on June 9, 2008, and April 30, 2009, by the appellee Tang Maolin. In the "Education Level" column, he filled in that he graduated from Xi'an Institute of Technology, intended to prove that Tang Maolin deceived the company regarding his educational background and continued to do so even after renewing the labor contract in 2008.

The appellee Tang Maolin argued in defense: 1. When he joined the appellant Guanlong Company, he had provided a false academic certificate as required by the company's recruiters. After August 2008, everyone at Guanlong Company, from top to bottom, was aware that Tang Maolin had provided a false academic certificate. Therefore, Tang Maolin was not promoted, which was Guanlong Company's handling of the matter. In December 2008, Guanlong Company renewed the labor contract with Tang Maolin, indicating that Guanlong Company, considering Tang Maolin's strong work capabilities, no longer held his forged academic qualifications against him. Meanwhile, Tang Maolin argued that during his employment, his work performance was always excellent and he fully possessed the capabilities required for the position. From the perspective of fairness and reasonableness, the labor contract could not be terminated solely on the basis of forged academic qualifications. In 2010, Guanlong Company terminated the labor contract with him on the grounds of forged academic qualifications, which was a pretext to purge long-serving employees. Guanlong Company should pay him compensation for illegal termination of the labor contract. 2. Regarding the RMB 15,000 advance payment slip, because the loan slip was not signed by the company's supervisor, Tang Maolin actually did not receive this sum, and he disagreed with returning it. Regarding the RMB 10,000 advance payment slip, Guanlong Company's operational procedures specified that there was a limit on business expense reimbursements for employees, which caused Tang Maolin's expense to not be reimbursed in a timely manner. Tang Maolin argued that the judgment of the court of first instance was appropriate and requested that the court of second instance dismiss Guanlong Company's appeal and uphold the first instance judgment.

The appellee Tang Maolin did not submit any new evidence.

The court found that the two sets of evidence above were supplementary to the evidence presented by the appellant Guanlong Company at the first instance. Although Guanlong Company only provided them during the second instance, the appellee Tang Maolin did not deny their authenticity. The court confirmed the authenticity of the above evidence.

After the second instance, the Shanghai No. 2 Intermediate People's Court confirmed the facts found in the first instance.

It was further found that Article 34 of the appellant Guanlong Company's *Employee Handbook* provided: "An employee who has any of the following circumstances seriously violating the company's rules and regulations shall be dismissed without any economic compensation: ... (2) Falsely reporting professional qualifications or other items of resume by fraudulent means..."

The issues in dispute in the second instance were: 1. Whether the appellant Guanlong Company lawfully terminated the labor contract with the appellee Tang Maolin, and whether Guanlong Company should pay Tang Maolin compensation for illegal termination; 2. Whether Tang Maolin should fully repay the two sums claimed by Guanlong Company.

The Shanghai No. 2 Intermediate People's Court held in the second instance:

The appellee Tang Maolin's conduct of providing a false academic certificate and making false statements at the time of joining the company clearly constituted fraud. However, whether Tang Maolin committed fraud when renewing the labor contract with the appellant Guanlong Company at the end of December 2008 was disputed. The key issue was whether Guanlong Company was aware of Tang Maolin's forged academic qualifications at the time of renewal and whether it made a false declaration of intent. First, Tang Maolin provided an audio recording of Ma Yuxin, attempting to prove that the company knew about his false academic certificate at the time of renewal. However, the recording contained many ambiguous phrases and was insufficient to prove that Ma Yuxin had informed Guanlong Company of Tang Maolin's forged academic qualifications. Second, Guanlong Company provided a written statement from Ma Yuxin stating that due to a job transfer, he did not report Tang Maolin's forged academic qualifications to the company and did not handle the matter. Although Ma Yuxin was a manager at Guanlong Company and had a certain interest in the company, this evidence was not the sole piece of evidence, and its probative value could be assessed in conjunction with other evidence. Third, the transfer order provided by Guanlong Company showed that Ma Yuxin had indeed been transferred to another post before Guanlong Company renewed the labor contract with Tang Maolin. Fourth, Tang Maolin still filled in the "Education Level" column on the personnel information card in 2009 as the Department of Materials Engineering of Xi'an Institute of Technology. A comprehensive analysis of the evidence submitted by both parties indicated that Tang Maolin had consistently concealed the fact that he provided a false academic certificate at the time of joining the company. Tang Maolin also had no evidence to prove that Guanlong Company had actually known about and forgiven his false academic certificate. Therefore, Tang Maolin still committed fraud when renewing the labor contract in December 2008. Articles 26 and 39 of the Labor Contract Law clearly stipulate that a labor contract entered into by fraudulent means, causing the other party to act contrary to its true intention, is void, and the employing unit may terminate the labor contract accordingly. Thus, Guanlong Company's termination of the labor contract with Tang Maolin had a legal basis, and it should not pay compensation for illegal termination of the labor contract. Furthermore, while China's labor laws fully protect the legitimate rights of employees, they also lawfully protect the employing unit's right to manage employment. It is an important means for an employer to manage its employees by imposing necessary constraints through corporate rules and regulations. Article 34 of Guanlong Company's *Employee Handbook* provided that an employee who falsely reports professional qualifications or other resume items by fraudulent means shall be dismissed without any economic compensation. During the trial, Tang Maolin did not dispute the authenticity of the *Employee Handbook*. Tang Maolin's conduct of providing a false academic certificate was also strictly prohibited by Guanlong Company's internal rules. Guanlong Company's termination of the labor contract with Tang Maolin based on its internal rules was an exercise of its management rights and was permissible. Moreover, Tang Maolin signed a *Commitment Letter on Taking Up Employment* in 2007, which stated: "As an employee of Shanghai Guanlong Valve Machinery Co., Ltd., I hereby make the following commitments: ... The personal materials I have previously provided to the company are true and valid. If any falsification is made, I am willing to be unconditionally dismissed..." This commitment letter was an agreement between Tang Maolin and Guanlong Company based on the principle of good faith, and Tang Maolin should have been aware of the legal consequences of breaching the agreed obligations. The agreement between the parties did not violate the law and was legally valid. Therefore, from the perspective of this commitment, Guanlong Company, upon discovering Tang Maolin's forged academic qualifications, had a basis for terminating the contract based on the commitment. As for Guanlong Company's claim that Tang Maolin signed dual contracts with clients to pocket the price difference, constituting a serious violation of rules, and its request to terminate the labor contract on that ground, due to insufficient evidence, this claim could not be sustained. However, this did not affect Guanlong Company's right to terminate the contract on the grounds that Tang Maolin forged his academic qualifications, deceived the company, and violated the Labor Contract Law, company rules and regulations, and his own commitment. The court of first instance's finding that Guanlong Company's termination of the labor contract with Tang Maolin was unlawful and that Guanlong Company should pay Tang Maolin compensation for illegal termination of the contract was improper and should be reversed.

Regarding the issue of whether the appellee Tang Maolin should return the disputed sums. The disagreement between the parties centered on whether the RMB 15,000 loan claimed by the appellant Guanlong Company was established, and whether the RMB 6,922 in vouchers submitted by Tang Maolin fell within the scope of expenses reimbursable by Guanlong Company. The parties should provide sufficient evidence to support their respective claims. With respect to the RMB 15,000 advance payment slip that Guanlong Company required Tang Maolin to return, based on the advance payment slip provided by Tang Maolin, it could be determined that Guanlong Company implemented a leadership approval system for advance payments, requiring the advance payment slip to be filled out and submitted for the supervisor's approval and signature. The advance payment slip for this loan was not signed by the supervisor. Guanlong Company also did not provide other evidence to prove that it had actually paid this sum to Tang Maolin. Therefore, the court of first instance had sufficient reason to find that the loan was not established. Regarding the RMB 10,000 business expense that Guanlong Company required Tang Maolin to return, expenses incurred by Tang Maolin for business purposes during his employment at Guanlong Company should have been reimbursed by the company. Tang Maolin had submitted evidence proving that he spent RMB 6,922 for business purposes, while Guanlong Company had no evidence proving that the above expenses were used for Tang Maolin's personal benefit. Therefore, Guanlong Company should reimburse the above expenses. Tang Maolin agreed to return the balance of RMB 3,078, which did not violate the law. Guanlong Company's refusal to reimburse on the grounds that business expense reimbursements must be submitted to the supervisor for review within one month after the expense was incurred lacked a legal basis. The court of first instance's fact-finding on these two sums was clear, and its judgment was appropriate.

Accordingly, pursuant to Article 153, paragraph 1, subparagraphs (1) and (3) of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, and Articles 26, paragraph 1 and Article 39 of the Labor Contract Law of the People's Republic of China, the Shanghai No. 2 Intermediate People's Court rendered the following judgment on July 25, 2011:

I. Affirm item 2 of the main text of the civil judgment of the Shanghai Jiading District People's Court (2010) Jia Min Si (Min) Chu Zi No. 418;

II. Reverse items 1 and 3 of the main text of the civil judgment of the Shanghai Jiading District People's Court (2010) Jia Min Si (Min) Chu Zi No. 418;

III. The appellant Guanlong Company shall, within ten days from the effective date of this judgment, pay the appellee Tang Maolin a differential in the fourth quarter bonus (commission) of 2009 in the amount of RMB 20,493.89, the first quarter bonus (commission) of 2010 in the amount of RMB 1,198.40, and the second quarter bonus (commission) of 2010 in the amount of RMB 32,213.00, totaling RMB 53,905.29;

IV. The appellant Guanlong Company's request not to pay the appellee Tang Maolin compensation for illegal termination of the labor contract is upheld.

This judgment is final.
